{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the preference is a 'pragmatic wager' maximizing 'informational content at risk' without relying on induction fails because acting on this wager to avoid future error implicitly assumes the future will resemble the past (uniformity of nature). If the laws of nature change arbitrarily tomorrow, the theory that was 'bold' and highly corroborated yesterday offers no rational advantage over a weaker one; thus, the decision to prefer it for future action smuggles in the very inductive assumption about temporal stability that Popper rejected.","premise":"Acting on a preference for a specific theory to guide future choices requires an assumption that the conditions which made it preferable in the past will remain relevant in the future.","premises_essential":["c4637e081c711c90b576f98c9da165937bce1ff9becb0a1e6321f52e30cd6a11"],"successor_question":"Can Popper distinguish between a methodological rule for learning and a practical rule for acting without presupposing that the future will resemble the past?","target":"c4637e081c711c90b576f98c9da165937bce1ff9becb0a1e6321f52e30cd6a11"}]}